    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1058">

                <pb facs="1106" n="986" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg986"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/1819: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Laughlin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-04-16T17:00:00+00:00">April 16, 1931—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 16—3:15 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>22. In a document which he gave to Count Romanones,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d1058-f1"/> the Bang defined his position. The paper was to be
                    published after the Monarch had quit Spain. I have a copy of this important
                    statement.</p>
                <p>Press correspondents have informed me that the document has been published in the
                    United States. Consequently, unless otherwise instructed, I shall not telegraph
                    it but send it by the next pouch. It is clear from the King’s definition of his
                    position that he has not “abdicated”. The Provisional Government obviously
                    desires to suppress it here. The Spanish press makes no mention of it but, on
                    the contrary, makes repeated references to the King’s “abdication”.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Laughlin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1058-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1058-f1-content">
                <p>Conde de Romanones (Alvaro Figueroa y Torres), Minister of Foreign Affairs In the
                    last Cabinet under King Alfonso XIII.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
